# The proposal column of test/katana/intent/carddemo-batch.review (R-066): a Fable advisor's reading of each
# fact, 2026-10-18, made from the evidence and the programs' source, as a probability and a reason, with the
# tests (T-) and documents (D-) that would settle it, catalogued in docs/research/ibm-options.md section 33.
# A PROPOSAL (R-031): it confirms nothing, and no figure counts it as a person's word (R-029).
# One line a fact: `PROGRAM | kind | subject | percent | reason | tests | wording`. The percent is the
# advisor's on every line. `wording` says whose words the reason is: `Fable`, the advisor's report verbatim;
# `Opus`, Opus's, written from the report (the advisor gave one reason a class of paragraph, or "as above",
# and Opus put it on each line; J-209). The advisor is a Fable session separate from the one that wrote R-066.
CBTRN02C | enum | APPL-RESULT: APPL-AOK, APPL-EOF | 40 | The program stores 8 into it before each file operation (MOVE 8 TO APPL-RESULT, lines 237, 255, ...), 0 on success, 12 on any other status, 16 on end of file; only 0 and 16 are named. A result code with four values, two named; as the kind is defined ("holds one of its condition-names' values") it is false. The rule is wrong here as stated; a fact "a status code whose values 0 and 16 are named" would be true. → T-E1 (will report 8 and 12 unnamed), T-E2 | T-E1, T-E2 | Fable
